  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protecting human rights- promoting economic and climate justice- Southern Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will be implemented from 1 September 2021 to 31 July 2023. It will be implemented in Zimbabwe, Malawi, Zambia, Mozambique and countries falling in the Sothern Africa Development Committee. Local communities and Indigenous peoples will be at the center of decision -making processes in relation to their lands and resources, and development priorities. The program will be in strategic partnerships with Regional entities like Southern Africa Development Committee Parliamentary Forum and its Executive Secretariat. The project is targeting the mining affected communities, Southern African citizens, civil society organizations, government, regional bodies, corporates and will aiming to reach a total of 5000 beneficiaries, Zimbabwe, Zambia, Mozambique over project period  It will be implemented through direct implementation and, in partnership with regional entities like Southern Africa Development Community (SADC) Parliamentary Forum, Southern Africa Development Community (SADC) Executive Secretariat, CSOs(Civil Society Organizations) and is aiming to  achieve the following outcomes by 2023 :  On Human Rights and Communities: People from extractive -industry affected communities &#x2013; especially women, Indigenous peoples and those most marginalized from decision -making processes &#x2013; are able to fully realize and defend their rights  -focusing on corporate awareness raising on Free and Prior informed Consent (FPIC) with regional bodies, large corporates and large CSO networks; gender equality and social inclusion training (GESI); and engaging governments and companies on results and their policy implications. With regard to Economic Justice and accountable Governance: Governments are more transparent and accountable in the generation, collection and expenditure of revenues from the extractives industries and they also maximize opportunities for local participation and linkages to wider economy - focusing on regional  information disclosure policies; consensus around prior</narrative>
  </description>
